# 5. The Three Dataset Splits ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

This is extremely important in real ML engineering.

Why shouldn't we train and evaluate a model on exactly the same data?

Because a model might memorize the training examples instead of learning patterns that generalize.

We divide data into three sets.

Typical 70 / 15 / 15 split

70%

Training

Learn parameters

15%

Validation

Choose settings

15%

Test

Final evaluation

### Training set

The model learns patterns and adjusts its parameters using this data.

### Validation set

Used to compare models, tune hyperparameters and make development decisions.

### Test set

Used for a final evaluation after model development decisions are complete.

Golden rule: Don't repeatedly use the test set to tune your model. That would make the test set part of the development process.

## Python implementation

```
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split# First separate the test setX_train_val, X_test, y_train_val, y_test = train_test_split(    X, y,    test_size=0.15,    random_state=42)# Then separate validation from remaining dataX_train, X_val, y_train, y_val = train_test_split(    X_train_val,    y_train_val,    test_size=0.1765,    random_state=42)print("Train:", X_train.shape)print("Validation:", X_val.shape)print("Test:", X_test.shape)
```

Why `0.1765`?

Because:

\\[ 0.1765\times0.85\approx0.15 \\]

So validation becomes approximately 15% of the original dataset.

`random_state=42` makes the random split reproducible.

