## Step 5 — Train a model

```
from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegressionmodel = LinearRegression()model.fit(X_train, y_train)
```

What happened?

```
X_train + y_train
        ↓
Linear Regression
        ↓
Learned weights and bias
```

## Step 6 — Make predictions

```
predictions = model.predict(X_test)print("Actual:", list(y_test))print("Predicted:", predictions)
```

## Step 7 — Evaluate

```
from sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_errorfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error, r2_scoreimport numpy as npmae = mean_absolute_error(y_test, predictions)mse = mean_squared_error(y_test, predictions)rmse = np.sqrt(mse)r2 = r2_score(y_test, predictions)print("MAE:", mae)print("MSE:", mse)print("RMSE:", rmse)print("R2:", r2)
```

### Understand the metrics

| Metric | Meaning                                                     |
| ------ | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| MAE    | Average absolute prediction error                           |
| MSE    | Average squared prediction error                            |
| RMSE   | Square root of MSE                                          |
| R²     | Relative goodness of fit against a mean-prediction baseline |

R² can be negative on test data when the model performs worse than predicting the test-set mean.

# 10. Beyond the First Model — What Should an ML Engineer Check?

Training a model is only one part of the job.

You should also ask:

1. Is the dataset representative of real-world data?
2. Are there missing or incorrect values?
3. Is there target leakage?
4. Is the model overfitting?
5. Is the chosen evaluation metric appropriate?
6. Does the model perform consistently across important customer groups?
7. How will predictions be served?
8. How will performance be monitored after deployment?

This is how you start developing an engineering mindset.

# 11. Interview Preparation 🎯

Q1. What is the difference between AI, ML and Deep Learning?

> AI is the broader field of building systems that perform tasks associated with intelligence. Machine Learning is a subset of AI that learns patterns from data. Deep Learning is a subset of ML that uses multi-layer neural networks.

Q2. Regression vs classification?

> Regression predicts continuous numerical values, while classification predicts discrete categories or class labels.

Q3. Why do we split a dataset?

> To train the model, make development decisions using validation data, and independently evaluate its final generalization performance using test data.

Q4. What is data leakage?

> Data leakage occurs when information that should not be available during model training or prediction influences the model, resulting in misleading evaluation or unrealistic performance.

Q5. What is generalization?

> Generalization is a model's ability to perform well on new, unseen data rather than only on the examples it was trained on.

Q6. What is the difference between a parameter and a hyperparameter?

| Parameter               | Hyperparameter                       |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| Learned during training | Set before or during model selection |
| Weights and bias        | Learning rate                        |
| Regression coefficients | Tree depth                           |
| Neural network weights  | Batch size                           |

